# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
# ==========================================================
#   SenseVoice text emotion labeller — GPU cluster image
#   (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct through vLLM, offline batch mode)
# ==========================================================
#
# Runs scripts/label_emotions_text.py, the transcript-only half of round 3's
# emotion pseudo-labelling. It shares nothing at runtime with the ASR training
# image (docker/Dockerfile.cluster): different model, different framework, no
# common code path beyond the Python standard library.
#
# Build and push (run from THIS directory; substitute your own hostname and
# Harbor project for the placeholders, exactly as in Dockerfile.cluster):
#
#   docker build --no-cache --platform linux/amd64 \
#       --build-context scripts=../scripts \
#       -t <cluster-registry>/<project>/sensevoice-textlabel:vllm0.27.1-v2 \
#       -f Dockerfile.textlabel .
#   docker push <cluster-registry>/<project>/sensevoice-textlabel:vllm0.27.1-v2
#
# Two things about that command differ from Dockerfile.cluster's and are not
# cosmetic:
#
#   --platform linux/amd64 is mandatory, not a convention. The cluster is
#   x86_64 H100 NVL; vLLM publishes a manylinux_2_28_x86_64 wheel and a separate
#   aarch64 one, so a build that inherits an Apple-silicon builder's native
#   platform would silently install the aarch64 wheel and produce an image no
#   node can run. Building on an x86_64 host is strongly preferred: under qemu
#   emulation the verification layers below execute ~300 MB of emulated native
#   extension code and take minutes rather than seconds.
#
#   --build-context scripts=../scripts exists so the build context stays this
#   directory. The verification layer needs one file from the repo
#   (scripts/label_emotions_text.py) and the repo root is 5.7 GB, most of it
#   outputs/ and .venv/ — with --no-cache that whole tree would be handed to the
#   daemon on every build. The named context transfers scripts/ (about 1 MB)
#   instead, and the file is bind-mounted for the check rather than COPY'd, so
#   it lands in no layer. If your Docker is too old for named contexts, build
#   from the repo root with `-f docker/Dockerfile.textlabel .`, change the
#   --mount below to `type=bind,source=scripts,target=/mnt/scripts` and add a
#   .dockerignore covering outputs/, .venv/, data/, models/ and .git/ first.
#
# The tag must stay in sync with whatever sbatch script requests this image, for
# the same reason Dockerfile.cluster documents: a stale tag that still exists in
# the registry fails quietly with old contents instead of loudly at pull time.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the tag is -v2, and why there is a fifth verification layer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# v1 built clean, pushed clean, and passed all four verification layers below.
# It then died on the node, inside engine start: vLLM and Triton compile their
# CUDA utilities with a *runtime* JIT that shells out to a C compiler, and
# nvidia/cuda:13.0.3-runtime-ubuntu24.04 ships no toolchain at all. The first
# thing that wanted cc was the first thing that ran on a GPU.
#
# The four layers did not miss this by bad luck. Every one of them tests an
# import, and the imports were genuinely fine — the image proved it could be
# *loaded*, which is not the property that failed. Nothing in a build that never
# touches a GPU has any reason to invoke a compiler, so the toolchain's absence
# had no way to show up before the node found it.
#
# Hence layer five, at the bottom, and hence the apt line below installing a
# toolchain into what is nominally a runtime image. Layer five does not ask
# whether a compiler exists — `which cc` proves a file is on PATH and nothing
# about whether it works — it compiles a translation unit, links it, runs it,
# and then compiles a real CPython extension through Triton's own build entry
# point with Triton's own include resolution. What still cannot be proved here
# is named in that layer's comment: everything past the point where the driver
# or a GPU is required.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this is a second image and not a few more lines in Dockerfile.cluster
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The conflict is in the dependency metadata, so no install order resolves it.
# Measured against vllm 0.27.1's declared requirements:
#
#   vllm 0.27.1 requires opencv-python-headless>=4.13.0
#   every opencv-python-headless from 4.13.0.90 up requires
#       numpy>=2 ; python_version >= "3.9"
#
#   vllm 0.27.1 requires numba==0.65.0
#   requirements.txt caps numba<0.61
#
# requirements.txt pins numpy<=1.26.4, and its scipy<1.14 / numba<0.61 /
# llvmlite<0.44 / scikit-learn<1.6 / umap-learn<0.5.8 caps exist for no other
# purpose than holding that ceiling (commit 319195d). `numpy<=1.26.4` and
# `numpy>=2` have an empty intersection, so one install set cannot contain both
# stacks — this is arithmetic, not a resolver preference.
# tests/test_textlabel_image.py pins that reasoning so a future attempt to merge
# the two images is told why it cannot before it spends a build on finding out.
#
# The torch situation is subtler than the numpy one and worth stating exactly,
# because it looks like a non-conflict today. vllm 0.27.1 pins torch==2.13.0,
# which is the very version Dockerfile.cluster installs and does satisfy
# requirements.txt's torch>=2.12.1. But it is an *equality* pin that moves with
# every vLLM release, and it comes with torchvision==0.28.0 and torchcodec>=0.14
# attached — so a shared image would hand control of the training stack's torch
# to whatever vLLM was compiled against next. Splitting costs nothing here: the
# labeller reads a manifest and writes a JSONL, and imports nothing from the
# training stack.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base image
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Deliberately NOT the site's NGC PyTorch mirror that Dockerfile.cluster uses.
# That base earns its place there by supplying a driver-matched CUDA userspace
# for a torch this project then has to replace anyway; here it would be pure
# cost. vLLM's wheels are compiled against exactly torch==2.13.0 and would
# replace NGC's custom-ABI torch 2.6 on the first pip invocation, so the whole
# reason to start from NGC evaporates, while its two documented defects (the
# .egg wheel tags that crash `pip check`, and the dead extra-index-url in five
# pip config paths) would come along for free.
#
# nvidia/cuda:13.0.3-runtime-ubuntu24.04 instead, for three concrete reasons:
#
#   1. CUDA major version. The PyPI torch 2.13.0 wheel that vLLM pins depends on
#      nvidia-cudnn-cu13, nvidia-nccl-cu13 and nvidia-nvshmem-cu13 — it is a
#      CUDA 13 build. Pairing it with a CUDA 12 base would be a mismatch this
#      project has no reason to introduce.
#   2. Forward compatibility with the node driver, which is the reason this is
#      a CUDA base and not python:3.12-slim. The node driver version is not
#      recorded anywhere in this repo; all that is known is that the training
#      image's cu126 stack runs there, which establishes r560-era at the low end
#      and says nothing about whether CUDA 13's own r580 requirement is met.
#      The CUDA 13 base installs cuda-compat-13-0 and its NVIDIA_REQUIRE_CUDA
#      declares driver families down to r535, so the cu13 wheels can run against
#      an older driver through the forward-compatibility path — which applies to
#      datacenter GPUs, and H100 NVL is one. A slim Python base carries no compat
#      libraries at all and would simply need the node driver to already be new
#      enough, discovered hours after the build passed.
#      That declaration has a second benefit: the container runtime refuses to
#      start on a genuinely too-old driver, which is the failure mode to want —
#      immediate and named, rather than a CUDA error deep inside engine startup.
#   3. Ubuntu 24.04 ships Python 3.12, inside vllm 0.27.1's >=3.10,<3.15 window.
#
# The weights are NOT baked in; see the staging note near the bottom.
FROM nvidia/cuda:13.0.3-runtime-ubuntu24.04

# Python plus the two small system pieces the wheels expect. The base is a CUDA
# runtime image, not a Python one, so nothing here is redundant with it.
#
# libgomp1 is cheap insurance: PyTorch's manylinux wheels vendor their own
# OpenMP runtime, but not every numeric wheel in vLLM's dependency tree does,
# and a missing libgomp.so.1 surfaces as an ImportError at engine start.
#
# python3-venv rather than python3-pip: Ubuntu 24.04 marks its system Python
# externally managed (PEP 668), so a system-wide pip install would be refused.
# A venv is the supported answer and also keeps every package this image adds in
# one directory that apt has no opinion about.
#
# build-essential and python3-dev are the v1 fix, and neither is a build-time
# convenience: they are what vLLM needs at *runtime*. Triton compiles its
# cuda_utils extension on first engine start with roughly
# `cc src.c -O3 -shared -fPIC -o mod.so`, and torch.compile's inductor backend —
# which vLLM's V1 engine turns on by default — shells out to a C++ compiler for
# its CPU-side codegen. The set actually required is therefore a C compiler, a
# C++ compiler, a linker, libc headers, and Python.h.
#
# build-essential rather than a hand-picked `gcc`, deliberately. It is gcc, g++,
# cpp, libc6-dev, binutils and make: roughly 250 MB installed, against an image
# already in the multi-GB range from the vLLM wheel and the nvidia-*-cu13
# runtime wheels, so about 2% for the whole toolchain. `gcc` alone would save
# most of that and would leave the inductor path broken — which is the same
# mistake as v1 one notch smaller, guessing which JIT path gets exercised first
# and spending a build-push-schedule-fail cycle to find out. The storage quota
# has the room (see the weights note at the bottom); another cycle does not.
#
# python3-dev is separate from build-essential and is not optional. Triton's
# generated source opens with #include <Python.h> and it resolves the include
# directory through sysconfig, whose venv scheme derives `include` from
# installed_base — that is /usr/include/python3.12, the *system* interpreter's
# headers, not anything inside /opt/venv. Ubuntu's python3 package does not ship
# them. Layer five checks that exact path rather than assuming this paragraph
# stays true across a base image bump.
RUN apt-get update \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        python3 \
        python3-venv \
        python3-dev \
        build-essential \
        libgomp1 \
        ca-certificates \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Everything below installs into the venv, and so does anything a future edit
# adds, because PATH puts it first. VIRTUAL_ENV is set so tooling that inspects
# it agrees with PATH.
ENV VIRTUAL_ENV=/opt/venv
ENV PATH=/opt/venv/bin:$PATH
RUN python3 -m venv /opt/venv \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip \
 && python -V \
 && pip -V

# The one install layer. vLLM and transformers are resolved together, in a
# single pip invocation, so the resolver sees both requirement sets at once —
# unlike Dockerfile.cluster, which is forced into two invocations by needing a
# different index for torch and therefore needs a separate metadata check to
# notice one install breaking the other.
#
# On the pins:
#
#   vllm==0.27.1 — exact, because the image tag names this version and an
#   unpinned install would make the tag a lie the moment upstream releases
#   again. The floor that the *code* requires is much lower: the labeller calls
#   LLM.chat(conversations, params, use_tqdm=False), so it needs a chat() that
#   takes a sequence of conversations and accepts use_tqdm, which is roughly
#   vllm>=0.6.0. 0.27.1 is simply the current release; the verification layer
#   below re-proves that API shape rather than trusting the version number.
#
#   transformers==5.15.0 — exact for reproducibility, not for compatibility.
#   What the labeller actually needs is a transformers new enough to carry the
#   Qwen2.5 chat template (>=4.45); vllm 0.27.1 already demands >=5.5.3, so the
#   real floor is set by vLLM and this pin only stops two builds of the same tag
#   from disagreeing. It is named explicitly rather than left to vLLM's
#   requirement because the chat template is this script's dependency, not
#   vLLM's, and a reader should be able to see that from this file.
#
# No PIP_CONSTRAINT file here, which is the other deliberate inversion relative
# to Dockerfile.cluster. There a constraints file defends a numpy ceiling and a
# torch pair against funasr's transitive dependencies. This image has no ceiling
# to defend: it *wants* whatever numpy and torch vLLM was compiled against, and
# constraining them would only give the resolver a way to produce a combination
# nobody tested.
#
# --no-cache-dir because the wheel set here is large enough (the vLLM wheel
# alone is 313 MB, before torch and the nvidia-*-cu13 runtime wheels) that a
# retained pip cache would be a visible fraction of the image.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir \
        vllm==0.27.1 \
        transformers==5.15.0

# Dependency-consistency check, carried over from Dockerfile.cluster unchanged
# in shape and narrowed to this image's packages.
#
# `pip check` is not used, but for a different reason than there. The .egg
# packaging defect that crashes it is NGC's and does not exist in this base, so
# `pip check` would in principle run here. It is still not the right check: it
# would police vLLM's entire transitive tree, ~60 direct requirements deep, and
# fail this build over upstream pins that are not ours to fix. So the check is
# aimed at exactly the packages this file installs, through importlib.metadata,
# which reads metadata and never parses wheel tags. Names are compared PEP 503
# normalised; requirements guarded by an extra we did not request are skipped.
#
# This layer and the transformers one below import `packaging`, which is never
# installed by name here. Its presence is not luck: transformers declares
# packaging>=20.0 and huggingface_hub declares packaging>=20.9, so it arrives
# with the install above or that install did not succeed. Worth stating because
# the base is a bare CUDA image and ships no Python packages of its own, so
# nothing in this file can be assumed present the way it could under NGC.
RUN python -c 'import importlib.metadata as md, re; \
from packaging.requirements import Requirement; \
ours=("vllm","transformers","torch","numpy"); \
norm=lambda n: re.sub("[-_.]+", "-", n).lower(); \
have={norm(d.metadata["Name"] or ""): d.version for d in md.distributions()}; \
need=[(p, r) for p in ours for r in [Requirement(s) for s in (md.requires(p) or [])] if not r.marker or r.marker.evaluate({"extra": ""})]; \
missing=["%s requires %s, which is not installed" % (p, r) for p, r in need if norm(r.name) not in have]; \
conflict=["%s requires %s, but %s is installed" % (p, r, have[norm(r.name)]) for p, r in need if norm(r.name) in have and not r.specifier.contains(have[norm(r.name)], prereleases=True)]; \
print("checked %d declared requirements of: %s" % (len(need), ", ".join(ours))); \
print("\n".join(missing + conflict) or "all declared requirements satisfied"); \
assert not (missing + conflict), "declared requirements of the installed packages are not satisfied; see the list above."'

# Runtime proof. Dockerfile.cluster's equivalent is what caught the missing
# torchaudio before a job was ever queued, and the argument for it is stronger
# here. Cluster turnaround is measured in hours and this labelling pass is a
# multi-hour job over ~550k lines, so an ImportError that waits for the
# scheduler already costs far more than a failed build — but on top of that the
# scheduler discards the batch script's exit code, so a job that died on an
# import is reported as a job that succeeded. The image cannot rely on the node
# telling anyone it was broken; these layers are the last point at which it can
# still say so.
#
# No GPU is present during a build, which is not a limitation for what needs
# proving. Everything below is import-time and signature-level:
#
#   1. numpy is on the 2.x side of the split. This is the whole reason the image
#      exists, asserted rather than assumed, so that anyone who later adds a
#      package that drags numpy back under the training stack's ceiling learns
#      it here.
#   2. `from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams` — the exact import the labeller's
#      backend performs on first use, and the one that would otherwise fail on
#      the node after the model was already being loaded.
#   3. The two call signatures the labeller depends on, checked by binding the
#      arguments it actually passes rather than by looking for parameter names.
#      That distinction matters: max_model_len is not a named parameter of
#      LLM.__init__ in 0.27.1, it is absorbed by **kwargs and forwarded to the
#      engine args, so a name-based check would fail on a call that works.
#      Binding also proves chat() still takes sampling_params second
#      positionally, which is how the labeller passes it.
#   4. SamplingParams built with the labeller's exact arguments — cheap, and it
#      is the one object the script constructs whose accepted fields have moved
#      across vLLM releases.
RUN python -c 'import importlib.metadata as md, inspect; \
import numpy as np; \
from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams; \
print("vllm", md.version("vllm"), "| transformers", md.version("transformers"), "| torch", md.version("torch"), "| numpy", np.__version__); \
assert int(np.__version__.split(".")[0]) >= 2, "numpy %s is below 2.x. That is the training stack ceiling (requirements.txt: numpy<=1.26.4), and vLLM cannot run under it; something in this image pulled numpy back down." % np.__version__; \
inspect.signature(LLM.__init__).bind_partial(None, model="/staged/models/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct", tensor_parallel_size=1, gpu_memory_utilization=0.9, max_model_len=2048, dtype="bfloat16", seed=0); \
print("LLM.__init__ accepts the labeller arguments"); \
inspect.signature(LLM.chat).bind_partial(None, [[{"role": "user", "content": "x"}]], SamplingParams(), use_tqdm=False); \
print("LLM.chat accepts (conversations, sampling_params, use_tqdm=False)"); \
p=SamplingParams(temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, max_tokens=8, logprobs=1); \
print("SamplingParams OK:", p.temperature, p.top_p, p.max_tokens, p.logprobs)'

# The chat template side, which the layer above does not cover. vLLM owns the
# model *implementation*; transformers owns the tokenizer and the chat template
# that turns the labeller's [{"role": ...}] messages into the token sequence
# Qwen2.5 was instruction-tuned on. A wrong or missing template does not raise —
# it produces a model that answers something other than a bare class name, which
# parse_response rejects, which becomes label: null, which the merge masks. The
# run then finishes, writes a complete-looking file, and supervises nothing.
#
# That is the failure this cluster is worst at surfacing, because the scheduler
# discards the batch script's exit code: a broken image reports a successful job.
# So transformers is imported for real rather than trusted from its metadata,
# and AutoConfig.for_model("qwen2") is asked to build the config class for the
# staged checkpoint's model_type (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct declares "qwen2" /
# Qwen2ForCausalLM). It raises ValueError on an unrecognised type, needs no
# weights and no network, and is public API — so it proves this transformers
# knows the architecture without pretending to prove the whole load path.
#
# The >=4.45 floor is the labeller's own requirement, checked separately from
# vLLM's much higher one so that the reason for it stays visible: if vLLM ever
# relaxes its transformers pin, this is the bound that still has to hold.
RUN python -c 'import transformers; \
from packaging.version import Version; \
from transformers import AutoConfig, AutoTokenizer; \
print("transformers", transformers.__version__); \
assert Version(transformers.__version__) >= Version("4.45"), "transformers %s predates the Qwen2.5 chat template (>=4.45 required by scripts/label_emotions_text.py)" % transformers.__version__; \
cfg=AutoConfig.for_model("qwen2"); \
print("qwen2 config class:", type(cfg).__name__); \
print("AutoTokenizer available:", AutoTokenizer.__name__)'

# The script itself. Bind-mounted from the named build context for the duration
# of this one command, so it leaves nothing in any layer — the copy that runs on
# the node comes from the repo bind mount, exactly as in Dockerfile.cluster.
#
# Worth being precise about what this proves and what it does not: the file
# checked here is the build-context copy, not the one the job will mount, so
# this verifies that *the image satisfies the script's imports*, not that the
# runtime copy is identical. That is the half that can go wrong silently.
#
# The laziness assertion is the interesting one. The labeller imports vLLM
# inside VllmLabeller._ensure_loaded specifically so the module, and its unit
# tests, stay importable without a GPU stack; if that ever regresses to a
# top-level import, every test machine without vLLM starts failing and the
# reason will not be obvious. Checking it here, where vLLM *is* installed and so
# a regression would otherwise pass unnoticed, is the cheapest place to catch it.
#
# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE keeps the check from trying to drop __pycache__ into
# the mounted context.
RUN --mount=from=scripts,target=/mnt/scripts \
    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python -c 'import sys; \
sys.path.insert(0, "/mnt/scripts"); \
import label_emotions_text as t; \
assert "vllm" not in sys.modules, "importing label_emotions_text pulled in vllm at module level; the backend import is supposed to stay lazy so the module and its unit tests run without a GPU stack."; \
msgs=t.build_prompt("test"); \
assert [m["role"] for m in msgs] == ["system", "user"], msgs; \
print("label_emotions_text imported OK | classes:", len(t.TEXT_CLASSES), "| default model:", t.DEFAULT_MODEL)'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer five: the toolchain, proved by using it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The four layers above prove imports; this one proves a compile. It exists
# because v1 passed all four and then died on the node for want of a C compiler
# (the incident is written up in the header). Two steps, deliberately separate.
#
# 5a is the floor and depends on nothing this image installs with pip: one
# translation unit through `cc`, linked to an executable, and then *run* — a
# compiler that produces a binary that will not execute is not a working
# compiler, and only running it says so. The same for `c++`, which torch.compile
# needs and which a hand-picked `gcc` would not have provided. Keeping this
# independent of vLLM matters: if a future edit breaks the toolchain in a way
# that also breaks importing triton, 5b would fall back to its own compiler
# invocation and could report the wrong cause. 5a cannot.
#
# The failure text is long on purpose. What the next person reads at 3am should
# name the missing thing and the reason it is needed, not leave them to infer it
# from a Triton stack trace.
RUN { echo 'int main(void){return 0;}' | cc -x c - -o /tmp/ccprobe && /tmp/ccprobe \
   && echo 'int main(void){return 0;}' | c++ -x c++ - -o /tmp/cxxprobe && /tmp/cxxprobe; } \
 || { echo "FATAL: no working C/C++ compiler in this image. vLLM does not need one to import, which is why every layer above passes without it, but Triton compiles its CUDA utilities with a runtime JIT that shells out to cc at first engine start, and torch.compile shells out to c++ — so the failure lands on the node, hours into the queue, and the scheduler reports the dead job as a successful one. The base is a CUDA *runtime* image and ships no toolchain; install build-essential in the apt layer near the top." >&2; exit 1; } \
 && rm -f /tmp/ccprobe /tmp/cxxprobe \
 && echo "cc and c++ compile, link and run"

# 5b goes after the same path Triton will take on the node, as far as that path
# can be walked without a GPU:
#
#   - the compiler *Triton* resolves, in Triton's order ($CC, then gcc, then
#     clang), rather than whatever `cc` happens to point at;
#   - the Python.h that Triton's own sysconfig lookup finds. That lookup is the
#     part most likely to rot: inside this venv the include path resolves to the
#     system interpreter's /usr/include/python3.12 (the venv scheme derives
#     include from installed_base), so it depends on python3-dev being present,
#     and Debian's posix_local scheme is remapped exactly as Triton remaps it;
#   - triton.runtime.build._build itself, so the real compiler invocation is
#     exercised rather than a reimplementation of it. The guard on that call is
#     the second thing this layer has had to learn. Its first version asserted
#     that _build's parameter names were a superset of the six it knew about and
#     then called it without ccflags, which this Triton requires: the guard
#     passed, the call raised TypeError, and the fallback was never reached. A
#     superset test answers "does this signature look familiar"; the question is
#     "can the call I am about to make be made". So the exact keyword set is
#     assembled first and put through inspect.signature().bind() — if it does not
#     bind, the layer says so loudly and compiles directly instead, degrading to
#     a weaker check but never to no check. ccflags and extra_compile_args are
#     passed as empty lists when the installed signature has them, which is what
#     Triton's own callers pass when they have no extra flags;
#   - a genuine CPython extension module, including <cuda.h> from Triton's
#     bundled backend headers when they are there, which is then loaded through
#     the import machinery. Compiling proves the headers parse and the compiler
#     driver works; importing proves the result matches this interpreter's ABI.
#
# What is NOT proved here, and cannot be at build time:
#
#   - the link against libcuda. Triton's real cuda_utils build passes
#     libraries=["cuda"] with library_dirs from `ldconfig -p | grep libcuda.so`,
#     and libcuda.so.1 arrives from the driver mount on the node. This probe
#     builds a shared object without -lcuda, which is why it links at all here.
#   - anything downstream of that: ptxas on a real kernel, cubin load, the
#     forward-compatibility path against the node's driver version. All of it
#     needs a GPU, so the first real evidence comes from the first job.
#
# The boundary is therefore: everything up to and including "this image can
# compile and load a CUDA-aware CPython extension" is settled here; everything
# that needs the driver is settled by the job.
RUN python -c 'import importlib.util as iu, inspect, os, pathlib, shutil, subprocess, sysconfig, tempfile; \
cc=os.environ.get("CC") or shutil.which("gcc") or shutil.which("clang") or shutil.which("cc"); \
assert cc, "no C compiler on PATH. Triton resolves one at runtime as $CC, then gcc, then clang, and compiles its CUDA utilities with it at first engine start; with none present this image fails on the node rather than here. Install build-essential."; \
scheme=sysconfig.get_default_scheme(); \
scheme="posix_prefix" if scheme == "posix_local" else scheme; \
pyinc=sysconfig.get_paths(scheme=scheme)["include"]; \
print("compiler:", cc, "| sysconfig scheme:", scheme, "| Python.h from:", pyinc); \
assert os.path.exists(os.path.join(pyinc, "Python.h")), "Python.h is not under %s, which is where Triton JIT-compiled extensions look for it (sysconfig scheme %r). Ubuntu ships those headers in python3-dev, not in python3." % (pyinc, scheme); \
tri=iu.find_spec("triton") and __import__("triton"); \
cudainc=[d for d in ([os.path.join(os.path.dirname(tri.__file__), "backends", "nvidia", "include")] if tri else []) if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, "cuda.h"))]; \
print("triton:", os.path.dirname(tri.__file__) if tri else "NOT INSTALLED", "| bundled cuda.h:", cudainc or "not found, probe compiles without it"); \
src="\n".join(["#include <Python.h>"] + ["#include <cuda.h>"] * bool(cudainc) + ["static struct PyModuleDef m={PyModuleDef_HEAD_INIT,\"ccprobe\",NULL,-1,NULL};", "PyMODINIT_FUNC PyInit_ccprobe(void){return PyModule_Create(&m);}"]); \
d=tempfile.mkdtemp(); \
p=os.path.join(d, "ccprobe.c"); \
pathlib.Path(p).write_text(src); \
mod=tri and iu.find_spec("triton.runtime.build") and __import__("triton.runtime.build", fromlist=["_build"]); \
build=getattr(mod, "_build", None); \
sig=inspect.signature(build) if build is not None else None; \
kw=dict(name="ccprobe", src=p, srcdir=d, library_dirs=[], include_dirs=cudainc, libraries=[]); \
kw.update({k: [] for k in ("ccflags", "extra_compile_args") if sig is not None and k in sig.parameters}); \
exec("def fits(s, kw):\n try:\n  s.bind(**kw)\n  return True\n except TypeError as why:\n  print(\"WARNING: triton.runtime.build._build exists but will not accept the call this probe intends to make (%s). Compiling directly instead, which still proves the toolchain but does NOT walk the Triton build path, so the strongest half of this check is not running. Update the keyword set above.\" % why)\n  return False\n"); \
usable=build is not None and fits(sig, kw); \
print("build route:", "triton.runtime.build._build(%s)" % ", ".join(sorted(kw)) if usable else "DIRECT INVOCATION of %s, the Triton build path was NOT exercised (see the warning above)" % cc); \
so=build(**kw) if usable else (subprocess.check_call([cc, p, "-O3", "-shared", "-fPIC", "-o", os.path.join(d, "ccprobe.so"), "-I" + pyinc] + ["-I" + i for i in cudainc]) or os.path.join(d, "ccprobe.so")); \
s=iu.spec_from_file_location("ccprobe", so); \
m=iu.module_from_spec(s); \
s.loader.exec_module(m); \
print("compiled a CPython extension with the JIT toolchain and imported it:", so)'

# Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct is roughly 28 GB in bf16 and is deliberately NOT baked
# in. The storage quota is not the binding reason — with the weights staged
# separately it has about 30 GB of margin, which is precisely the margin that
# baking them in would spend. What settles it is that a layer that size is
# re-pulled by every node that runs this image, and that weights living in a
# layer can only be changed by rebuilding and repushing the whole thing, which
# couples the model version to the image version for no benefit.
#
# They are staged on the shared filesystem instead and passed by path, following
# the same convention as the acoustic labeller's emotion2vec directory:
#
#   #CONTAINER -v /home/share/<user>/models:/staged/models
#
#   python scripts/label_emotions_text.py \
#       --manifest /corpus/vn/train.jsonl \
#       --model /staged/models/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct \
#       --out /outputs/emo_text.jsonl
#
# Do not mount them under /data. The site mounts a local scratch filesystem
# there after the bind mounts are applied, so the mount silently resolves to
# someone else's directory — documented in docs/chunk_training.md, and it fails
# invisibly rather than loudly.
#
# The sbatch script also needs `#CONTAINER --shm-size 32G` if it ever runs with
# --tensor-parallel-size > 1: vLLM's workers are separate processes that talk
# through shared memory, and Docker's 64 MB default is not enough for them.

# A local path is the expected --model, so nothing should reach the hub, and a
# compute node with no egress turns an accidental hub call into a hang rather
# than an error. Failing fast is better; override with `-e HF_HUB_OFFLINE=0` on
# a node that does have egress and genuinely wants to download.
ENV HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1

# No ENTRYPOINT: the site sbatch wrapper passes --disable-entrypoint, so one set
# here would be bypassed and would only mislead whoever reads this file.
#
# Nothing from the repo is COPY'd — it is bind-mounted at runtime by the job
# script, so no credentials and no stale code are baked in.
WORKDIR /workspace
